Title: PHP, libapreq-1 and CVE-2026-109618
ACL: joe=rw, *=r
status: draft

Rasmus Lerdorf personally [ported libapreq-1](https://github.com/php/php-src/blob/master/main/rfc1867.c) to the PHP runtime decades ago. **That port remains secure**. **CVE-2026-109618** is a novel, [sophisticated attack on the PHP runtime itself](https://calif.io/php2shell/) (misuse of `longjmp()` under OOM), exacerbated by the browser industry's unwillingness to meaningfully secure the file upload process by otherwise providing human users with file upload performance metadata widgetry in the browser.

Cynically, but honestly, this is simply because they don't want your bug reports about non-performing uploads. Sadly, abrogation of responsibility informs most of the division of labor (yours, or mine) in protocol standards development.

This missing functionality puts the burden of responsibility where it never belonged: on server-side API hooks into the in-flight upload apparatus itself.

@sunstarsys/status/2113323849794380204

### NOTE

AI did not **discover** this set of PHP vulnerabilities. PHP developers have been managing this risk for over 20y largely by ignoring it until someone could demonstrate an operational exploit for it.

This is the new new that the NSA tacitly admits being in possession of given the chicken little calls it has made publicly about the significance of the present moment:

@NSACyber/status/2112903220209098897

AI vendors also seem confident they have one; however there's no evidence of a massive successful internet attack based on these fears. It's been mostly a giant nothingburger, which justifies the PHP developers' stance over the full lifetime of the known vulnerability situation.

Nevertheless, an important mitigator for Linux for this class of vulnerabilities is to set `memlimit=-1` in your php configuration, and on the host run

  ```shell
    sysctl vm.overcommit_memory=1
  ```

as root, so your PHP webserver process gets OOMKilled by the kernel instead of receiving soft allocation errors on `realloc(3)`, which woould otherwise trigger the problematic `longjmp(3)`...

Why should you do this, regardless of whether you have already patched? Because the initial patch only covers **round one**. The next rounds are just beginning [CVE-2026-115560](https://www.php.net).

I also recommend ignoring the siren song of the AI industry about formal methods being significant ameliators for detecting/managing this kind of architectural defect. This is a system-logic error that I don't believe is easily captured by the kind of type analysis formal methods rely on.  The industry is welcome to prove me wrong by counter-factual evidence on this precise situation, but I won't be holding my breath.


